# Cloudflare Pages custom headers.
#
# This file must live in the build output directory (the Pages project is
# configured with root directory `pages`, output directory `public`), so it
# sits alongside index.html. It is applied by Pages itself — it does not
# affect the poller worker, which sets its own headers in code.
#
# Syntax is finicky: a path pattern on its own line, then each header
# indented beneath it. A header value cannot be wrapped across lines, which
# is why the CSP below is one very long line.
#
# The CSP is the real reason this file exists. sanitizeHtml() in app.js
# strips dangerous markup out of feed content before it is injected into the
# reading pane, but that is a blocklist, and blocklists lose races. The
# policy below is the backstop: even if something slips past the sanitizer,
# the browser refuses to execute it.

/*
  Content-Security-Policy: default-src 'self'; script-src 'self'; style-src 'self' 'unsafe-inline' https://fonts.googleapis.com; font-src 'self' https://fonts.gstatic.com; img-src 'self' https: data:; media-src 'self' https: data:; connect-src 'self' https://*.workers.dev; object-src 'none'; frame-src 'none'; child-src 'none'; worker-src 'none'; manifest-src 'self'; base-uri 'none'; form-action 'self'; frame-ancestors 'none'; upgrade-insecure-requests
  X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff
  Referrer-Policy: strict-origin-when-cross-origin
  Permissions-Policy: accelerometer=(), camera=(), geolocation=(), gyroscope=(), magnetometer=(), microphone=(), payment=(), usb=()
  Cross-Origin-Opener-Policy: same-origin

# Why each CSP directive is what it is:
#
#   script-src 'self'
#     The whole point. index.html has no inline <script> and no event
#     handler attributes, so no 'unsafe-inline' or hashes are needed, and a
#     javascript: URL or injected <script> in feed content cannot run even
#     if the sanitizer misses it. Do not add 'unsafe-inline' here — it would
#     give back exactly the capability this file exists to remove.
#
#   style-src 'self' 'unsafe-inline' https://fonts.googleapis.com
#     'unsafe-inline' is a deliberate concession: feeds routinely ship
#     style="..." attributes on article markup, and blocking them makes
#     posts render badly. CSS injection is a much smaller problem than
#     script injection, and sanitizeHtml still removes <style> elements
#     outright, so what this actually permits is inline style attributes.
#     googleapis.com is the @import at the top of style.css, and also the
#     one host a theme may name in its `fontImport` field — themes.js
#     rejects any other origin at validation time precisely so a custom
#     theme's web font fails with a clear message instead of a console CSP
#     error nobody will connect back to the theme file.
#
#   font-src https://fonts.gstatic.com
#     Where that Google Fonts stylesheet fetches the actual font files.
#
#   img-src / media-src 'self' https: data:
#     Article images and podcast audio come from whatever host the
#     publisher uses, so these have to stay open. data: is here because the
#     sanitizer deliberately permits inline raster images (but not SVG,
#     which can carry script). http: is omitted — the page is HTTPS, so
#     mixed content would be blocked anyway.
#
#   connect-src 'self' https://*.workers.dev
#     'self' covers the /api/* Pages Functions. The wildcard covers the
#     manual-refresh POST to the poller worker, whose URL the user sets in
#     Settings. If you ever move the worker behind a custom domain, add it
#     here or the Refresh button will start failing with a CSP error in the
#     console rather than an obvious network error.
#
#   object-src / frame-src / child-src / worker-src 'none'
#     Nothing in this app embeds plugins, frames or web workers, and feed
#     content has no business doing so either.
#
#   base-uri 'none' and form-action 'self'
#     Defense in depth alongside the sanitizer, which already strips <base>
#     and <form>. An injected <base> could repoint every relative URL on the
#     page; an injected form could post wherever it liked.
#
#     form-action is 'self' rather than 'none' on purpose. The Settings and
#     New Folder modals are <form method="dialog">, and browsers have not
#     been consistent about whether form-action applies to dialog-method
#     submission — a stricter value risks breaking the only UI for setting
#     the worker URL, which is not worth the small margin. 'self' still
#     blocks the actual threat of a form posting to another origin, and
#     feed-injected forms are removed by the sanitizer before this matters.
#
#   frame-ancestors 'none'
#     No one may frame the reader — clickjacking protection, and stricter
#     than the legacy X-Frame-Options.
#
# If the app breaks after deploying this, the browser console names the
# exact directive that blocked the request. To roll back without redeploying
# the whole project, rename the directive to
# Content-Security-Policy-Report-Only, which reports violations instead of
# enforcing them.
